/** Manual quality-pass translations siman 125 — overrides auto-generated text */
export const MANUAL = {
  'baer-heitev': {
    '1#א': `"What remained." Shach challenged: for above in siman 124 seif 14 he ruled that what remained is permitted; and one may ask — for nitzok applies only to wine forbidden in benefit, as below in siman 126 seif 5; and here, since what goes out is forbidden only for drinking, why is what remained in the utensil forbidden because of nitzok? — requires study. (And it appears to me for understanding: the Mechaber holds that lifting is different, for it is his manner to libate thus — even though he did not agitate it we say regarding it nitzok is connection, and they made lifting like the issur of drinking as agitation for issur of benefit; and if so the Mechaber's words are correct only regarding Rama — one may challenge why we forbid in siman 124 what went out for drinking — requires study.) And Shach wrote: for us everything is permitted in case of loss, for all that is forbidden by law for drinking — since gentiles in this time are not idolaters — is permitted even for drinking, as in siman 124 seif 64:`,
    '1#ב': `"In case of great loss." Shach wrote, and it appears to me that until here Rama did not write thus except by law; but for us it is permitted here even not in a case of great loss:`,
    '1#ג': `"He lifted." Here Taz and Shach challenged Rama in what he wrote that if he did not lift the vessel everything is permitted; and above in siman 124 seif 14 we say when the non-Jew removed the tap it is forbidden because of his ko'ach — however Shach wrote that for us everything is permitted in case of loss:`,
    '2#_': `B'dieved. But l'chatchila it is forbidden even with ten forces and more — thus it appears in Tur and the poskim:`,
    '3#_': `"By himself." And even if the Jew by himself is not able, it is permitted b'dieved; but l'chatchila it is forbidden even if the Jew is able and the non-Jew is not able — as is found in Rashba:`,
    '4#_': `"Permitted." (Since without this the wine would also have flowed and the act of the non-Jew would not have helped except that through it it flows more nicely — if so it is permitted) because it is assistance that has no substance. Tosafot:`,
    '5#_': `"In his anger." In Perishah he challenged: for the Mechaber wrote above siman 124 seif 9 that it is forbidden for drinking, and Taz also challenged thus; and in his view what the Mechaber wrote here is primary, for the ruling there is against the mishnah, as he wrote in that siman — see there:`,
    '6#_': `"That dripped." In Levush he wrote that even if there was clear wine there that was already drawn off, nevertheless it is permitted since his intention was to pour the grapes; and Taz challenged him and concludes that if there was pure clear wine there, even b'dieved it is forbidden — see there:`,
    '7#א': `"Water." And even one who holds nitzok is connection — that is specifically wine to wine, but water to wine not; and accordingly, even if it touched water it is permitted. Shach:`,
    '7#ב': `"To dilute." And even in a barrel, when it is known that he intends to dilute it — it is forbidden. Shach:`,
    '7#ג': `"Permitted." For it is a rabbinic safek to be lenient; and Ran wrote that in a cup, even if he poured only a little water or too much, it is forbidden — since people's manner differs in dilution between much and little, it is called dilution, end of his words; and it appears that in these countries where they do not dilute in a cup it is permitted unless it is known that his intention was for dilution; and if he knew that it was wine, he presumably intended to dilute it. Shach:`,
    '8#א': `"What goes out." It appears that what is inside is permitted — and see above s.k. 1. Shach:`,
    '8#ב': `"Becomes forbidden." According to Rama above siman 124 seif 8 one must say that it deals with here that he lifted the vessel and they agitated it, for otherwise there is no agitation in the vessel; and Taz wrote that for us there is no issur here, for we permit contact of a non-Jew through something else, as he wrote in siman 124:`,
    '9#_': `"Lacking." Shach wrote — and that is when it is sealed, for otherwise it is forbidden because of agitation, as above siman 124 seif 8; and even Rama agrees here since he removed the spout, that is, the lifting; and Taz wrote that even though it is open he does not forbid here, for there is no manner of libation in this since he carries it on his way, and we are not concerned lest he touched the mouth of the spout when it is narrow:`,
    '10#א': `"Permitted here." And the reason is brought in Shach: since here the vessel is wide, for this reason we are concerned when it is full lest he touched it — and therefore when it is lacking it is permitted; since it is wide he does not want to agitate it lest it spill — and therefore even if he lifted it, it is permitted here; and above siman 124 seif 8 that permits only if he agitated without lifting deals with a narrow vessel:`,
    '10#ב': `"On a pole." For then it is far from him and he cannot touch it. Tur:`,
    '10#ג': `"Provided." Mordechai wrote: and if by tilting wine splashed onto the non-Jew and then splashed back into the wine it is permitted, for there is no manner of libation in this:`,
    '11#_': `"And as he walks." Shach wrote: if it was dripping a little, even if he knows that it is dripping it is permitted; except that Rashba wrote that one should resolve the matter — and for us it is obvious that it is permitted:`,
  },
  'siftei-kohen': {
    '1#א': `["And what remained in the utensil is forbidden etc. He challenged: for above in siman 124 seif 14 he ruled that what remained inside is permitted — and that is because they were not stringent regarding his ko'ach in nitzok; and likewise below in seif 8 he brought the language of Tur that what goes out is forbidden for drinking, and it appears that what is inside is permitted; and one may ask — for nitzok applies only to wine forbidden in benefit, as below in siman 126 seif 5; and here, since what goes out is forbidden only for drinking, why is what remained in the utensil forbidden because of nitzok? And likewise he challenged the Rav — requires study. And for us everything is permitted in case of loss, for all that is forbidden by law for drinking — since gentiles in this time are not idolaters — is permitted even for drinking, as above in siman 124 seif 64:"]`,
    '1#ב': `["Unless there is great loss. He challenged: for even without this it is permitted, and even in minor loss, as he wrote above in siman 124 seif 64, for the reason that gentiles in this time are not idolaters and all their contact is without intention — and one is forced to say that his ko'ach is different, for it was explained there that all that is forbidden by law for drinking is for us entirely permitted; and furthermore Rabbeinu Tam and his companions hold that contact of a non-Jew through something else is considered his ko'ach, which forbids by law only for drinking — and we hold not like Rabbeinu Tam in this, as explained in Beit Yosef siman 124 and in Shulchan Aruch there seif 11, that his contact through something else is lesser than his ko'ach and is forbidden even in benefit — and nevertheless we hold above in siman 124 seif 64 that contact of a non-Jew through something else in this time is permitted even for drinking — if so, all the more so his ko'ach; and one must say that the Rav did not write thus except by law, and there is a practical difference where it is known that the gentiles are idolaters, even not to publicize the matter, as he wrote there in hagahah that one does not publicize the matter before gentiles — and so in several places one must say thus according to the Rav z"l; but for us it is permitted here even not in a case of great loss; and likewise in Sefer Afrei Rabbati, beginning of daf 80, it appears to be lenient in this time since they are not accustomed to libate, even without great loss:"]`,
    '1#ג': `["Or that he did not lift the vessel etc. Requires study in this law, for what Beit Yosef brought in siman 124 from Rambam and Rosh (regarding one who pours and the other receives) and Ran, and after him the Rav followed, does not appear at all from their words — see there; and likewise it appears in Teshuvot HaRashba daf 131 side 1 that he wrote in the name of Ramban that if the non-Jew removed the cover from the barrel, even though the wine is not agitated at all in this, the wine is forbidden for drinking — for it is like one who pours and the other receives, and it is his ko'ach of the non-Jew with intention that is forbidden for drinking, until here is his language; and likewise above siman 124 seif 14 they forbade when the non-Jew removed the tap because of his ko'ach; and even though Bach wrote that there it is different, for we consider removing the tap as if he touched the wine through something else and agitated it, end of his words — he challenged: for if so, why is what remained inside permitted? And furthermore, even if the tap does not touch the barrel at all but is inserted in another tap, the law there is thus — see there; rather we consider it his ko'ach, as Taz wrote there explicitly; and furthermore, if so here too we would consider it as if he touched the wine itself and agitated it; and Darkei Moshe resolved that with the tap it is different, for it is his manner thus to remove wine from the barrel, end of his words; and this too is strained, for likewise it is his manner thus to pour without lifting; and furthermore, what difference is there here and there — his ko'ach is the same; and Bach challenged in a later pamphlet on this distinction that one must say whether it is his manner thus or not; and also what Beit Yosef wrote there that Rambam explained non-Jew adna that the non-Jew lifted the barrel — he challenged: non-Jew adna appears to mean he tilted the barrel; and likewise in Tosafot perek R. Yishmael (Avodah Zarah daf 60 side 1) s.v. non-Jew adna, and in Mordechai there in what he wrote: if there is a little wine in the barrel that must be lifted from one side — forbidden, end of his words — it appears that even if he did not lift the barrel but only tilted it on its side it is forbidden because of his ko'ach — see there; and so it appears explicitly from the words of Rashba regarding Yisrael adna and non-Jew on a wine vat — forbidden in what they explained there in the name of Raavad, because when he tilts the vat he increases the pouring of wine from the barrel; and all the more so according to what Ran explained there similarly — see there; and further proof from below seif 4 — investigate, for I was brief here; for us, since we hold that gentiles in this time are not idolaters, everything is permitted in case of loss:"]`,
    '2#_': `B'dieved etc. But l'chatchila it is forbidden even with ten forces and more — thus it appears in Tur and the poskim:`,
    '3#א': `["Permitted b'dieved. But l'chatchila it is forbidden even if the Jew is able and the non-Jew is not able — thus it appears in Rashba, and likewise in Darkei Moshe:"]`,
    '3#ב': `["If the non-Jew was able by himself etc. And even if the Jew by himself is not able — permitted b'dieved — thus it is in Rashba in Teshuvot HaRashba, and Beit Yosef brings it; and likewise it is the view of Ran; and Darkei Moshe wrote: and some say regarding a minor, that since it is evident that the non-Jew is primary — it is forbidden:"]`,
    '4#_': `"More nicely — permitted." Since without this the wine would also have flowed and the act of the non-Jew would not have helped except that through it it flows more nicely — if so it is permitted, for it is assistance that has no substance. Tosafot:`,
    '5#_': `"In his anger etc." For it is contact of a non-Jew without intention through something else (as explained above siman 124 seif 64 that it is permitted for drinking) — Rosh; and in Perishah he challenged: for the Mechaber wrote above siman 124 seif 9 that it is forbidden for drinking, and Bach also wondered at this and resolved with difficulty — see there:`,
    '6#_': `"Permitted b'dieved." Since it was not yet drawn off in the winepress, as above siman 123 seif 46; and l'chatchila it is forbidden — lest pouring of pure wine — Rashba and Ran in the name of Raavad:`,
    '7#א': `["Permitted even for drinking. And even one who holds nitzok is connection — that is specifically wine to wine, but water to wine not. Tur, and likewise Rosh and Rashba; and accordingly there is a practical difference that even if it touched water it is permitted:"]`,
    '7#ב': `["And he did not know that it was wine. But if he knew that it was wine, he presumably intended to dilute it; and likewise Ran wrote that in a cup the matters appear thus — that even if he poured only a little water or too much it is forbidden, since people's manner differs in dilution between much and little it is called dilution, end of his words; and it appears that in these countries where they do not dilute in a cup it is permitted unless it is known that his intention was for dilution:"]`,
    '7#ג': `["But if he intends to dilute etc. As Beit Yosef — it deals with a cup; but in a barrel Ran and Rashba wrote that even if he intends to dilute it is permitted, since it is an uncommon matter the Sages did not decree at all, end of his words — and this is strained; and also in Ran and Rashba it does not appear at all except that they wrote that even if he put water in the measure of proper dilution for drinking it is permitted, since there is no manner of diluting a barrel in one act, and in an uncommon matter the Sages did not decree at all — meaning that presumably we are not concerned for dilution; and since he did not intend to dilute it is permitted, for an uncommon matter the Sages did not decree; but when it is known that he intended to dilute — it is forbidden:"]`,
    '7#ד': `["Forbidden for drinking. Because he is likely to touch:"]`,
    '7#ה': `["Permitted. For it is a rabbinic safek to be lenient. There:"]`,
    '8#א': `["What goes out is forbidden. It appears that what is inside is permitted — and see above s.k. 1:"]`,
    '8#ב': `["The wine becomes forbidden. According to the Rav above siman 124 seif 8 one must say that it deals with here that he lifted the vessel and they agitated it, for otherwise there is no agitation in the vessel:"]`,
    '9#א': `["And he holds the mouth of the spout. As Beit Yosef — and that is when it is sealed, for otherwise it is forbidden because of agitation, as above siman 124 seif 8; and even the Rav agrees here since he removed the spout, that is, the lifting:"]`,
    '9#ב': `["Permitted. And we are not concerned lest he touched the mouth of the wineskin when it is narrow:"]`,
    '10#א': `["Permitted here. Meaning: here, since the vessel is wide, for this reason we are concerned when it is full lest he touched it — and therefore when it is lacking it is permitted; as Ran wrote, since it is wide he does not want to agitate it lest it spill — and therefore even if he lifted it, it is permitted here; and above siman 124 seif 8 that permits only if he agitated without lifting deals with a narrow vessel (and what was written here in Shulchan Aruch — Rashba, Raavad, and Rosh — is an error; investigate):"]`,
    '10#ב': `["On a pole with two etc. — permitted. For it is far from him and he cannot touch it. Tur:"]`,
    '10#ג': `["Provided etc. And if by tilting wine splashed onto the non-Jew and then splashed back into the wine it is permitted, for there is no manner of libation in this. Mordechai there, and Darkei Moshe brings it — meaning that it splashed onto the body of the non-Jew; but if onto his garment, without this it is obvious that it is permitted, for it is contact of a non-Jew through something else without intention, as above siman 124 seif 64:"]`,
    '11#_': `"And as he walks etc." Tur wrote in the name of Rashba: if it was dripping a little, even if he knows that it is dripping it is permitted, for the wine is not poured out by the ko'ach of gentiles and he does not increase the pouring in its swaying — for even if it were placed on the ground it would drip and emit as one who drips and emits on his shoulder, and there is no ko'ach of the non-Jew here at all — and therefore it is permitted; except that one should resolve the matter, until here is his language; and for us it is plain that it is permitted:`,
  },
  'beur-hagra': {
    '1#א': `"He took etc." — daf 60 side 2.`,
    '1#ב': `"For drinking." Rosh there daf 60 in the name of Rashi, and Ran there in the name of Rashi, and he transgressed Avodah Zarah there; but in our Rashi there he wrote that it is forbidden in benefit; and likewise at the end of the chapter in the conclusion of the matter he wrote "koso" etc.; and so too Tosafot there daf 60 s.v. non-Jew etc.; and Tosafot 58 side 2 s.v. "ela" etc.:`,
    '1#ג': `"And what etc." Because of nitzok; and even though there side 2 (side 2) we say "digavai" etc. — that is for what was thought to reject Rav Huna. Rashba and Shitah Mekubetzet; and likewise in Tosafot 56 side 2 s.v. "gergutni" in the name of Rashi; and likewise Rambam, as he wrote in siman 124 seif 8 "he took" etc. "becomes forbidden"; but Rashi there side 2 (s.v. digavai etc.) and Tosafot there s.v. kocho etc. (did not write thus); and likewise Rosh and Mordechai; and this is what "some say" etc.:`,
    '1#ד': `"There is etc." He ruled entirely like the first reasoning, except that in great loss he ruled that nitzok is permitted in all places, as he wrote above siman 126; and this is what he wrote below etc.:`,
    '1#ה': `"Or that not etc." As he wrote above siman 124 seif 8 — and it is puzzling, as he wrote; and likewise I found in Shach here that he wondered at this — and see there seif 14 and there seif 64 in hagahah, wine that was placed etc.; and below seif 4; and in Tosafot in several places:`,
    '2#_': `"And if it is etc." But Tosafot there s.v. "hava" etc., and Rosh in his rulings there, and Mordechai, and Rashba, and Shitah Mekubetzet forbid even with many forces:`,
    '3#א': `"If ko'ach etc." Tosafot daf 60 s.v. non-Jew etc., and s.v. "hava" etc. — as he wrote side 2 side 2: Rava said to those etc. — l'chatchila he speaks; and likewise Shitah Mekubetzet, not like Raavad who forbids even b'dieved; and Avodat HaTaharah 137 side 2: (Lekut) if etc. mixed together etc. — to exclude the view of Ran who wrote according to his explanation what he wrote daf 60 non-Jew adna etc., that even if the forces are not mixed together, and even this one is able and this one is not — see there (until here):`,
    '3#ב': `"And even etc." Rashba and Tur and Tosafot mentioned above; and in Beit Yosef on this there are several views — see there:`,
    '3#ג': `"And he etc." Tosafot there s.v. "hava" etc., and there side 2 s.v. "amar" etc., and Shitah Mekubetzet:`,
    '4#א': `"If the wine etc." In the aforementioned Tosafot:`,
    '4#ב': `"But etc." See there in Tosafot:`,
    '4#ג': `"Non-Jew etc." As above s.k. 1 — his ko'ach without etc.:`,
    '5#א': `"He threw etc." Tosafot there s.v. "kol" etc., and s.k. 7, and Rif and Rambam omitted it, for they hold the halachah is not like R. Eliezer — R. Eliezer for his reason holds there side 1 in the latter wording that even his ko'ach is permitted; and the same applies to throwing; but Rashba challenged: on the first wording what is there to say? — and he resolved like Tosafot:`,
    '5#ב': `"Even for drinking." There in Tosafot, and proof from that he challenged "in his anger there is not" etc.:`,
    '5#ג': `"And if he did etc." Mishnah there:`,
    '6#א': `(Lekut) "That there is wine in it etc." According to R. Yitzchak in Tosafot there s.v. "mah hu" etc.; and likewise Ran explained in the name of others; and he wrote that what he wrote "even though the wine drips" etc. deals with wine in the winepress; and he wrote that so too in the Tosefta as we learn there: a non-Jew who brought up grapes in baskets and small tubs to the winepress — even though he crushed them in the winepress and wine drips onto the grapes — it is permitted; and he wrote: but Raavad explained that the concern is because the wine in the baskets and small tubs is poured by the non-Jew [and because of his ko'ach, and this is the view, and see above Magen Avraham] in pouring the grapes into the winepress; and even though it is not wine that was not yet drawn off, nevertheless l'chatchila it is forbidden because of "go, go" etc.; and according to this view Raavad ruled that if a Jew assists the non-Jew it is permitted even l'chatchila, since in pure wine it is not forbidden except l'chatchila because of a decree, as he wrote above in seif 3 — it is a decree upon a decree; and this is what he wrote "even though there is" etc.; and if etc.; and Ran wrote: according to Raavad's explanation, even for our winepress where wine does not float on the grapes — l'chatchila forbidden; unlike Tosafot's explanation; and likewise there is a practical difference regarding assistance, as above (until here):`,
    '6#ב': `"Even though there is etc." Gemara there and Tosafot there s.v. "mah hu" etc.:`,
    '7#א': `"Non-Jew etc." Tosafot daf 60 side 2 s.v. "kol" and Shitah Mekubetzet; and they brought in Teshuvot HaRashba 136 side 2 proof from the Yerushalmi as we learn there: R. Yirmiyah in the name of R. Chizkiyah — wine that they diluted hot is forbidden, cold permitted; "May I be punished if I did not do so since the days of R. Yosei" etc.; and even though he said "May I be punished" etc.; and likewise in our Gemara they did not distinguish, because they are also accustomed to drink cold; and because "do not distinguish" — nevertheless we hear that in such a case it is certainly permitted; and likewise Rosh and Shitah Mekubetzet; and this is what he wrote "and he" etc.; "but" etc.; and as he wrote 58 59: (Lekut) non-Jew threw etc. — as he wrote in the Yerushalmi: R. Shimon taught in the name of R. Avahu: throwing does not forbid stam yeinam; and Tosafot 59 side 2 s.v. "mah hu", Maggid Mishneh, and Shitah Mekubetzet (until here):`,
    '7#ב': `"And if it is not etc." For it is a rabbinic safek. There:`,
    '8#_': `"What goes out etc." Language of Tur, and according to his view that he ruled in s.k. 1 like the view of Rashi and Tosafot:`,
    '9#_': `"And he etc." See above siman 124 seif 17:`,
    '10#א': `"And not etc. and some say etc." As he wrote above: (Lekut) "he was" etc. — as he wrote above; and if he tilted etc.; and in a lacking vat it is permitted there, for he does not want to because it spills. Ran: "And some say" etc. — and that of tilting in a full vat, lest he touched, in the language of the first in Rashi and Or Zarua in the name of Raavad who explained on side 1 (until here):`,
    '10#ב': `"And if the Jew etc." Therefore they said there "he goes behind him":`,
    '10#ג': `"And if he was etc." Tosafot there s.v. "zika" etc.:`,
    '11#_': `"Non-Jew etc." As he wrote 58 side 2; and therefore in that case of the vat they were not concerned lest it spill, even though according to Rashba and Shitah Mekubetzet in such a case even what is inside is forbidden, as above s.k. 1: (Lekut) non-Jew etc. — Hagahot Avraham chapter 5 s.k. 27 s.v. Rashbam etc., and workers etc. (until here):`,
  },
  'mateh-yehonatan': {
    '1#_': `(Siman 125 in Shulchan Aruch seif 1) "A non-Jew took a utensil of wine etc." And what remained in the utensil also because of nitzok — see Shach s.k. 1, what he challenged from that above in siman 124 seif 14 that he ruled that what remained inside is permitted, and that is because they were not stringent regarding his ko'ach in nitzok; and see above what Azariah de Rossi wrote to resolve this challenge; and what he further challenged from below siman 126 seif 5, that we say that wherever it is forbidden only for drinking we do not say nitzok is connection — and here too it is forbidden only for drinking, as the Mechaber wrote — if so nitzok is connection does not apply; and it appears to me that according to reasoning from the Torah one should distinguish regarding wine inside the utensil whether below it is forbidden in benefit or is forbidden only for drinking — for if it is connection and is as if everything is mixed, what he wrote: issur of benefit or issur of drinking; and that Rashba wrote, and also Mechaber below siman 126 seif 5, that one who draws from it is permitted — that is since there is no liquid of the non-Jew touching in the utensil, and it is forbidden only because they said it is forbidden to use a utensil of wine of a non-Jew, as explained in Gemara and poskim — if so, well: where its issur is because of mixture and stam yeinam, then we properly say nitzok is connection and it is as if everything is mixed; unlike here, where it is forbidden because the utensil is of stam yeinam — therefore it is forbidden to use it, and in such a case we do not say nitzok is connection, for what was above would be considered as if below in a utensil of stam yeinam — therefore what was above was not forbidden; unlike here, where the wine was forbidden because of his ko'ach and what was above is forbidden because of mixture etc.:`,
    '2#_': `(There in hagahah) "And some say that we do not forbid" etc. This law is from Rosh; and see in Beit Yosef that he challenges the words of Rosh against each other — for in perek HaSocher et HaPo'alim siman 25 he wrote that because of nitzok it is not forbidden, for they were not so stringent regarding the ko'ach of a non-Jew to forbid what is connected to him through nitzok; and in perek R. Yishmael siman 4 he brought in the name of Rashi, and these are his words: even according to those who say nitzok is connection, nevertheless nitzok bar nitzok is not connection etc.; and if so, if the non-Jew emptied wine from a pitcher to a cup, the wine in the cup is forbidden because of the ko'ach of the non-Jew, and wine in the pitcher is forbidden because of nitzok; and if afterward a Jew drew wine from the barrel to the pitcher, the wine in the barrel is permitted, end of his words — if so Rosh holds that wine in the pitcher is forbidden because of nitzok, even though wine in the cup was forbidden only because of his ko'ach — from this we learn that he holds that nitzok applies also to his ko'ach; and one may resolve that certainly Rosh holds that wine in the pitcher is also not forbidden, for we do not say nitzok is connection in his ko'ach; and that which he wrote there "according to Rashi" — that is because he challenged from the Torah to distinguish between his ko'ach and other matters; and one must say: since his ko'ach is lighter than other stam yeinam and we do not say nitzok is connection where the issurim are lighter — Rosh challenged from where did he learn this; for this reason he brought the incident of Rashi that they did not forbid nitzok bar nitzok — and that is since wine forbidden because of nitzok is a lighter issur and we do not say nitzok is connection — if so, the same applies to wine forbidden because of his ko'ach — investigate [and what he wrote in his language there that wine in the cup is forbidden — he did not cite only Rashi's language, but he himself did not hold thus — investigate]:`,
    '3#_': `(There) "Nevertheless one should be stringent unless there is great loss." See Shach s.k. 2, what he challenged: for even without this it is permitted even in minor loss, since gentiles in this time — all their contact is without intention, as above; and one may resolve: well there in siman 124 seif 24 it deals with contact — one may well say thus, that the whole reason for contact with stam yeinam being forbidden is because he intends in his contact to libate — if so, for contact of a non-Jew without intention, why did they forbid? — and one must say the reason they forbade without intention is lest with intention; if so, in this time where there is no intention of libation at all, it is entirely permitted in minor loss; unlike ko'ach — they did not forbid because of intention of libation, but rather the Sages forbade all wine that comes from the ko'ach of a non-Jew with intention — if so, what difference to me in their days, since in any event it comes from his ko'ach with intention — forbidden even in this time:`,
  },
  'nekudot-hakesef': {
    '1#_': `(Siman 125 in Taz s.k. 2) "As he wrote in siman 124 seif 23, what I have" etc. Thus one must say, and he meant in that letter there; and I already wrote there that there is no difficulty:`,
    '2#_': `(There s.k. 4) "It is not precise" etc. For understanding: here too he speaks — unless there is great loss it is permitted without this, as below in siman 126:`,
    '3#_': `(There s.k. 10) "Explanation of Beit Yosef" etc., and it is explained in Ran and Rashba etc. — meaning as Shach wrote in s.k. 12:`,
  },
  'pitchei-teshuva': {
    '1#_': `"And if he agitated it." See Taz s.k. 12; and see in responsum Noda BiYehudah second series Yoreh De'ah siman 70 that he wrote that what Taz wrote — "and according to all the explanations there is no issur here" etc. — that is all three views that he brought earlier, and not like the view of the Gaon Maharam Berabi — see there:`,
  },
  'rabbi-akiva-eiger-yd': {
    '1#_': `(Siman 125 seif 1) Forbidden for drinking. It appears there is proof for this view from that incident of the child who permits treading in the press at his hand; and nevertheless it is his ko'ach because of the treading, as we say in Me'itzarta, and everything is forbidden because of nitzok in his ko'ach; one must say that his ko'ach is forbidden only for drinking; and for Rashba who holds that ko'ach is stam yeinam and also holds that we say nitzok in his ko'ach — it works out well, for he holds that all nitzok has no issur of benefit, being only as mixed, permitted except for the value of stam yeinam in it; but for Raavad who holds that nitzok is stam yeinam it is difficult at first glance, and one must say: since the Jew treads with him it is ko'ach of a Jew and ko'ach of a non-Jew mixed; however I do not know in such a case where they tread on the body of the grapes, as we say — and he touched with his foot — whether it is called ko'ach of a Jew and ko'ach of a non-Jew mixed, for nevertheless in that place where the non-Jew treads from those grapes wine drips from his ko'ach alone, and at first glance it is not called mixed except that both of them poured together, or that they loaded the beam onto the grapes together — requires study for the halachah; and behold according to Rambam that his ko'ach is forbidden in benefit, as he wrote in Mishneh Torah — I am very puzzled by the words of Rambam, and it is the language of the Mechaber above (siman 123): non-Jew who treads etc. forbidden for drinking — and requires great study:`,
    '2#_': `(Seif 6) "And if a Jew assists." In the source of the law in Teshuvot HaRashba and Teshuvot HaRosh it appears to me for practical halachah that it is explained that this is when there is no wine in the winepress, but it is forbidden from concern for wine on the grapes in the small tubs that substitutes for pure wine — in this, if a Jew assists, it is permitted, since even in pure wine by law it is permitted through ko'ach of non-Jew and Jew mixed in it; but in wine in a pit that is forbidden according to Ramban lest he touch with his hand the wine in the pit — in this there is no advantage in the Jew assisting — see there; and if so the Mechaber who mentions a winepress in which there is wine — it appears he holds like Ramban, and on this he says "and if a Jew assists" — requires study:`,
    '3#_': `(Taz s.k. 9) "And in Derishah he understood" etc. — "that it is contact through something else." He lacks understanding; and it appears there is a textual error here etc. Taz's intent: for even in water itself he did not touch except through what he threw into the pit, and it is only his ko'ach; and if so, even regarding nitzok water would have the law of wine; nevertheless we do not say his ko'ach in nitzok; one must say it deals with throwing the water by hand; and behold according to Rashba, and it is the view of the Mechaber (siman 126), that nitzok is connection is not like touching all of it, only as if it is mixed — if so certainly there is no nitzok in water — what difference does it make that it is as mixed:`,
  },
  'yad-ephraim': {
    '1#_': `"And what remained." Avodat HaTaharah and in responsum Noda BiYehudah: a non-Jew took wine from one barrel and filled others; and his taking was through a tube called a "heiber" — he drew wine from the barrel with it and lifted it and placed it for another barrel to drip there until it filled; and so he did for the entire barrel — he filled from the first barrel; and while he still placed wine to drip to the barrel he did not remove his hand below from the hole in the tube entirely, lest the splash be thicker than the hole of the barrel mouth — and therefore he held his finger there loosely so that a thin stream would come out; and he wrote to permit: for touching the stream is not like touching all the wine — for one who holds nitzok is connection; and even for one who does not hold nitzok is connection, it applies specifically when the splash goes down to the barrel and afterward he touched the splash; unlike here, when at the time he removed his finger from the first barrel he placed his finger in the tube below, and then it was not yet opposite the second barrel, and at the time of the splash to the second barrel there is no new contact, only because of the looseness the splash goes down on his finger — in any event it is not considered touching all of it, only nitzok; and there is room to permit through sixty against the wine in the tube, especially in a small splash in the issur into the heter; and see there that also regarding the wine in the tube itself there is room to argue whether it is forbidden in benefit — for one may say it is tircha, as he wrote siman 124; and from the aspect of drawing wine in the tube, raising is not called his ko'ach, only through that he draws air from inside the vessels the wine must come up to fill the voids from the air, and the wine is not drawn from the ko'ach of the person drawing, only the wind etc.; and therefore since the wine in the tube is nullified in sixty, there is room to permit even for drinking even without loss, since Rashba and Mechaber explicitly permit — see there:`,
    '2#_': `"The wineskin full or lacking." See Bach and responsum Avkat Rochel siman 62: a vessel full of wine that was placed on the board, and under this vessel was another vessel full of wine; and one non-Jew was walking in the house, and as he walked on the head of the board the upper vessel swayed from the swaying of the board and spilled and fell into the lower vessel — the wine is permitted even for drinking, for that which the wine was agitated by his ko'ach is permitted, as we say regarding the vat; and also what fell through swaying into the lower vessel was not forbidden, for even if he threw a pebble in his hand it would not be forbidden — see there:`,
  },
  'turei-zahav': {
    '1#א': `"And he lifted it and the wine went out etc." Beit Yosef in siman 124 brought these words of Rambam and wrote that they are from what they said in perek R. Yishmael — non-Jew adna, Yisrael akova — forbidden, for when wine comes it comes from the ko'ach of the non-Jew; and it appears to Rambam that specifically when the non-Jew lifts the vat and pours the wine, but if he does not lift it but only tilts it on its side and pours the wine into another vessel it is permitted; and likewise it appears from Ran who wrote that he takes the barrel in his hand etc.; and afterward he brought the words of Rosh who also wrote — evidently the essence of libation is in lifting and pouring etc. I wonder — for above in siman 123 Tur and Rama wrote that if a non-Jew treads wine, even though he did not touch with his feet, wine that comes from his ko'ach is forbidden; and this is an explicit gemara in perek R. Yishmael (Avodah Zarah daf 60) — in his ko'ach all disagree that it is forbidden, and there he did not lift anything at all. And further — what he wrote in siman 124 seif 14, that if the tap does not pass through all the walls the wine that goes out is forbidden because of the ko'ach of the non-Jew — and there is no lifting; and Darkei Moshe wrote on this challenge, and one may say that perhaps there it is different, for it is his manner thus to remove wine from the barrel — until here; and this is nothing — for if the manner of libation is only in lifting specifically, what do we care for the manner of removing wine thus? And in any event there is no manner of libation in this; and even if this answer is true, the first challenge is not resolved, for there wine is not relevant at all until after it comes from his ko'ach. Maharach wrote that in removing the tap the reason is not because of his ko'ach but we consider it as if he touched the wine through something else — and he was not precise, for Tur and Shulchan Aruch explicitly wrote that it is his ko'ach if it does not pass through all the walls; and in truth every contact through something else is called ko'ach, as Beit Yosef wrote explicitly in siman 124. And further — we find explicitly in Tosafot perek R. Yishmael (Avodah Zarah daf 60): one should be stringent with people when they tilt the vat that has a little wine to one side, that a non-Jew should not tilt it etc., because of the ko'ach of the non-Jew; and likewise in Hagahot Asheri; and if there is a little wine in the barrel that must be lifted from one side — forbidden etc.; and likewise in Mordechai; and likewise the import of Tur's words here, that he wrote the law of the ko'ach of a non-Jew who pours from vessel to vessel and did not mention that lifting is required. And further proof from what Mordechai wrote in the name of HaRif, which Rama brought nearby, that if the non-Jew shakes the hand of the Jew it is forbidden where it would not have flowed without this, even though he did not lift anything; and what Beit Yosef brought as proof from that Rambam mentions here "he lifted" — it appears because of the end he mentions it, that he concluded there "he lifted and did not agitate and did not touch — permitted"; and therefore he also mentions at the beginning "he lifted" — that even though he lifted, agitation is required; and therefore one must say thus: if lifting specifically forbids, why did he mention at the end that if he did not lift, even though nitzok is permitted — and this is a greater novelty than what he wrote "he lifted" at the end and did not agitate and did not touch — permitted, that is without pouring; and this is proof for the explanation of Rambam's words that I mentioned, that there is no answer to them and one cannot challenge from what Rambam wrote chapter 12 of Avodah Zarah: a non-Jew who touched wine and did not intend for this wine is permitted in benefit — how so? For example he fell on a wineskin of wine, or he reached his hand to a barrel intending it was oil and it was wine and the wine came from his ko'ach without intention — since he did not touch the wine it is permitted for drinking — how so? For example he lifted a barrel of wine and poured to another vessel and he thought it was beer — behold this is permitted, end of his words — we learn that we require lifting for a stringency, for in a leniency he said thus — even if he lifted and poured it is permitted in this. And what Beit Yosef brought from Ran — plainly there is no proof from there that Ran intended only to reveal that the non-Jew did not intend — the non-Jew drew from the vessel, as Beit Yosef himself wrote; and that which he mentions "the non-Jew took the barrel in his hand and pours" is not specifically thus but he mentions the usual manner, that the manner is to take in his hands when he pours; and what he brought as proof from Rosh who mentioned lifting and pouring in haste — he did not examine this well, for Rosh challenged Rabbeinu Tam who says the pouring of the non-Jew forbids only because of his ko'ach — and that is for drinking specifically — why should it not be full libation since there is lifting and pouring as one who libates fully? And on this he resolved that not in his presence it is not the manner of libation — until here there is no issur except because of his ko'ach; and in truth in his ko'ach we do not require even lifting, for the issur of his ko'ach is because of "go, go we say to the nazir" etc., as Rashi explained in perek R. Yishmael (Avodah Zarah daf 60); and if so, what is this that Beit Yosef wrote that from Rosh's words it appears there is no manner of libation without lifting — do we forbid because of libation? We forbid it only for drinking and it is because of his ko'ach and we do not care about libation — until here it appears Beit Yosef did not write these words except as pilpul and not to establish the halachah thus; and therefore he did not establish thus in Shulchan Aruch, and Rama who emended here at the end of the seif "or that he did not lift the vessel" did not trouble to examine fully but relied on what Beit Yosef wrote; and one should not be lenient at all here in the ko'ach of a non-Jew even without lifting, for nevertheless there is ko'ach of a non-Jew here — thus it appears to me plainly:`,
    '1#ב': `"Forbidden for drinking." On what he wrote siman 124 s.k. 30 — I have great wonder at this regarding Tur and likewise regarding Shulchan Aruch:`,
    '1#ג': `"And some say we do not forbid etc." Beit Yosef likewise in the name of Rashi, and he challenged from what Tosafot and Rosh wrote — the incident of Rashi with a non-Jew who poured into the cup etc. — we learn that the pitcher is forbidden because of nitzok to the ko'ach of the non-Jew; and it appears to me that one who examines Asheri plainly has no difficulty, for he did not write this in the name of an incident but wrote: Rashbam wrote in the name of Rashi that one may learn from here that even according to those who say nitzok is connection, nitzok bar nitzok is not connection etc.; and if so, if the non-Jew emptied wine from a pitcher to a cup and the wine in the cup is forbidden because of nitzok etc., and if afterward a Jew drew from the barrel to the pitcher, what is in the barrel is permitted, for it is nitzok bar nitzok, end of his words — behold all these matters follow those who say nitzok is connection, and that is the view of Raavad and his companions who hold nitzok is connection in all places even in the ko'ach of a non-Jew; and that of Rav Sheshet who permits nitzok to the ko'ach of a non-Jew is a mere rejection, as Beit Yosef wrote in his name — automatically there is nitzok to the ko'ach of a non-Jew, nevertheless what is in the barrel is permitted for all, for it is bar nitzok; and this is the main novelty to permit bar nitzok even for one who holds there is nitzok in the ko'ach of a non-Jew; and likewise in Tosafot the intent is thus — they first wrote "even for one who says nitzok is connection," and near this they wrote "an incident came before Rashi" etc., when the wine in the cup was forbidden — meaning, for that view it is forbidden, but in any event what is in the barrel is permitted as I mentioned; but Rashi himself holds that even nitzok is permitted in this:`,
    '1#ד': `"Nevertheless one should be stringent unless there is great loss." This language is imprecise — for in great loss, even in full nitzok, that is wine forbidden through contact of a non-Jew, we are lenient, as he wrote in siman 126:`,
    '1#ה': `"And if it is without intention etc." In the gemara it is stated that even smell decides the matter — that is, if it is old wine he certainly knew it was wine by smell:`,
    '3#_': `"And even if the non-Jew was able by himself to pour etc." Explanation: and the Jew is not able by himself to pour — thus Rashba in Teshuvot HaRashba; and on this Tur wrote in the name of Rashba that even a non-Jew assists the Jew — why is the non-Jew made primary etc. Explanation: even though the non-Jew has greater force, nevertheless now that the Jew also bears in his force, who will tell us that in that force needed for this carrying the non-Jew's share is greater than the Jew's, since in this carrying both have force? Ran too ruled to be lenient when the non-Jew is able and the Jew is not — and all this is specifically b'dieved, but l'chatchila it is forbidden:`,
    '5#א': `"Forbidden even in benefit." Beit Yosef wrote in the name of Rosh: that which is forbidden not in his anger — we are concerned lest the non-Jew touched the wine and libated; but in his anger we are not concerned lest he touched — and even though he touches, it is contact of a non-Jew without intention through something else and permitted for drinking. Alternatively, not in his anger is contact of a non-Jew with intention through something else and forbidden in benefit, for the Sages did not permit from his hand etc.; and Beit Yosef wrote "and our master wrote like the latter language," end of his words — explanation of his words: according to the first reason the primary issur is lest he touched with his hand — automatically if there is certainty he did not touch with his hand it is permitted even not in his anger; and according to the second explanation, also in this case not in his anger is forbidden, for it is contact with intention through something else; and Tur also wrote "and it pushes until it fell into the wine — forbidden" etc. — it appears that from the aspect of this pushing it is forbidden and not because of lest he touched with his hand — it is found there is stringency according to the second explanation; and Maharach understood that Beit Yosef meant to say there is a distinction between the two explanations of Rosh — according to the first reason there is no issur of benefit, and on this he wrote that Beit Yosef was not precise, for also according to the first reason it is forbidden in benefit; and in truth he did not descend to the truth of Rosh's words but there is a practical difference between them, as he wrote; and likewise it appears from Beit Yosef's words that he brought the language of Rashba who holds like the latter wording, as I mentioned who wrote "and he agitated the wine in the vessel" etc.; and there is another practical difference between these explanations — for us we hold contact of a non-Jew with intention through something else is permitted, as Rama wrote in siman 124; if so, according to the first explanation it is forbidden even for us lest he touched with his hand; but according to the second explanation the primary concern is contact through something else, which is permitted for us — it is found there is greater stringency in the first explanation; and therefore it appears one should be concerned for the first explanation and forbid even for us when one throws not in his anger and the object rolls and pushes it to the wine, whenever it is not clear he did not touch with his hand:`,
    '5#ב': `"And if he did so in his anger etc." This contradicts what Shulchan Aruch itself wrote siman 124 seif 19, and that ruling he established from himself, as I wrote there that it is against the mishnah; and Maharach wanted to resolve that Rambam explains what they said in the gemara "it goes from it" — that is specifically regarding a barrel which is heavier since it is a heavy object and touching is more relevant than with a stone or object; and therefore in his anger forbidden for drinking and not in his anger forbidden even in benefit — and he was not precise, for explicitly in the mishnah we say there was an incident and they permitted it — it appears even for drinking; and further in the gemara Rav Papa asks (on this) from that of the barrel in siman 124 — why not answer that the barrel of siman 124 is different, for it is heavy and touching is more relevant than with a stone or object:`,
    '6#_': `Permitted b'dieved. But l'chatchila not; and they said in the gemara the reason is because of "go, go we say to the nazir" etc.; and Rashba wrote in Teshuvot HaRashba HaAruch: Raavad explained the reason — wine is squeezed in small tubs, and when the non-Jew pours grapes in the winepress wine also splashes through his hand; and because of this l'chatchila it is forbidden, for they decreed lest pouring of pure wine; but b'dieved permitted; and even though much wine splashes on the grapes they did not decree b'dieved, because he does not intend in that pouring for pouring wine but for pouring grapes mixed in them that were not yet tread and wine was not yet drawn from them, and there is no manner of libating thus — until here is the explanation of his words: since when he pours grapes automatically wine among them also splashes — l'chatchila they decreed here because of "go, go" etc., that is lest he pour grapes since he also pours clear wine among them; and even though there is no issur here, for there is no wine on him, nevertheless they decreed lest another time he pour pure clear wine; and nevertheless b'dieved it is permitted since he does not intend at all, even for that wine — for if he intended for that wine it would be fit to decree because of other pure wine; and likewise it would be fit to forbid even if he did not intend to pour wine, for example if there was pure drawn wine in a special place there it would be fit to decree because of pouring wine alone — until here there is none of these; and likewise is Tur's intent in copying Rashba's words who wrote "because the grapes were not yet tread and their wine was not yet drawn and he does not intend to pour wine but to pour grapes" — meaning he does not intend to pour that wine itself that is mixed and there is no pure wine at all — until here permitted b'dieved; I wrote this because Levush and Maharach explained Tur's words in what he wrote "and he does not intend to pour wine" for its own reason — that is, permitted since his intention is on pouring grapes and not on the wine — it is ko'ach without intention through something else which is permitted; and accordingly even if there was pure wine there, that is already drawn — permitted; but this is not so, for the language of Teshuvot HaRashba that I mentioned does not appear thus at all; and also from the law the matter is plain — if there was pure clear wine there, even b'dieved it would be forbidden even if he intends to pour grapes, for this is worse than what we find nearby in the next seif regarding dilution with water, which is forbidden even b'dieved, for the reason is because it has closeness to wine they decreed more — all the more so here where there is greater closeness since it carries the wine itself and engages in its work they were more stringent than other things forbidden because of a decree — thus it appears to me for practical halachah plainly. And Rashba wrote there on this that Ramban explained the reason not like Raavad but lest the non-Jew touch the wine in the winepress; and there is a practical difference between the explanations — our winepress where there is no wine floating on the grapes: for Raavad forbidden because of clear wine, and for Ramban permitted — until here Tur first wrote therefore a non-Jew who brought etc.; and nevertheless l'chatchila forbidden to do lest he touch wine — that is Ramban's reason which is primary, as Rashba also wrote in Teshuvot HaRashba; and accordingly in our winepress permitted; and afterward he brought in the name of Rashba "some ruled" etc., that is Raavad; and accordingly also in our winepress forbidden; and in Perishah he wrote that what Tur first wrote regarding baskets deals with perforated ones where no wine collects at all, and in this all agree permitted b'dieved, unlike small tubs where wine remains inside from what is squeezed from the grapes, and therefore some forbid, as Rabbi wrote nearby, end of his words. And his honor rests in his place, and we say when he sleeps and lies he said this word — that there is no issur b'dieved at all for all views even in small tubs, and it is an explicit gemara that specifically l'chatchila is forbidden; and what Tur wrote in the name of Rashba "some ruled that it is forbidden" etc. — that is l'chatchila; and what he explained that baskets means perforated — this is a proper explanation, for not in vain did Tur first mention baskets and afterward small tubs, but Tur teaches us another practical difference between the explanations: according to Ramban's explanation that the reason is lest he touch wine in the winepress — l'chatchila forbidden even in perforated baskets, unlike according to Raavad's explanation where afterward the issur is only in small tubs, that is not perforated; and it is found that each explanation has stringency and leniency — for Ramban there is stringency l'chatchila even in perforated ones and leniency in our winepress where no wine floats on grapes, and for Raavad the reverse; and it appears there is another practical difference — when the non-Jew carries grapes in unperforated baskets to another vessel that is not a winepress: for Raavad forbidden and for Ramban permitted; and likewise it appears from Beit Yosef; and in Semak he concluded that what Tosafot wrote that the issur is specifically for a covered winepress is Rashba's explanation, that is in the name of Ramban — it appears for Raavad forbidden even if not covered, and it is fit for us to adopt the stringency of all explanations, and likewise appears Tur's view. And according to this exchange, if there are two leniencies — perforated baskets and our winepress — permitted even l'chatchila, except one who wishes to be stringent may say because "do not distinguish" of the Sages; but it appears to me plain what I wrote; and that Tur did not write l'chatchila forbidden also when throwing a stone in the winepress — there is no relevance to l'chatchila — are we dealing with fools that a Jew would leave a non-Jew to throw a stone in his wine? And in vain Perishah wrote it also deals with throwing a stone; but the matter is plain that whatever the Jew needs to do an action for the winepress he should distance that act from being done by a non-Jew because of "go, go" etc.: And what he wrote permitted even for drinking — the reason is he does not touch the wine with his hand only through the water, and in a moist object touching is not considered touching; and also nitzok is connection does not apply here, for they said connection only wine to wine and not water to wine; and it appears plain that this law deals even with throwing water by hand directly; and even though Beit Yosef wrote in the name of Ran "it is no worse than his ko'ach without intention" etc. — he did not call this ko'ach (are we?) but because the contact of the non-Jew is not in the wine it is no worse than other places where we are lenient in the ko'ach of the non-Jew; and therefore they needed here to say that water in wine is not considered nitzok; and in Derishah he understood it deals here specifically with throwing the water through a vessel; and because of this he troubled to resolve why the reason that there is no nitzok here — behold it is permitted without this, for it is contact through something else without intention; and in truth he troubled in vain on this:`,
    '7#א': `"Such as throwing water into a large barrel." Beit Yosef explained that even if he intends to dilute, in this it is permitted since it is an uncommon matter the Sages did not decree — end of his words; and this is explained in Ran and Rashba — and specifically in a cup where the manner is to dilute they forbade even a little water, because of a decree lest he dilute wine in water or lest he touch the wine itself since he is accustomed to it — thus Rashba and Ran; and even though we see now he did not touch, nevertheless we are concerned lest another time he not be careful at all since he accustomed himself to this; and therefore it is not comparable here to what Tur wrote end of siman 128 that whatever we are concerned lest he touched is forbidden even in benefit — for there we are concerned lest he touched now; and in vain Maharach troubled to resolve this challenge: And what he wrote "but if he intends to dilute — forbidden for drinking" because of the decree I mentioned, and Beit Yosef wrote in the name of Rashba and Ran — and even though in the previous seif they were lenient b'dieved, here regarding dilution in a cup it is fit to decree more since it has closeness to him; but in carrying grapes to the winepress it does not have such closeness etc.; and from this I learned nearby that if in those grapes there is pure wine already drawn, also there forbidden even b'dieved even though without intention — and even for us who are lenient now because gentiles are not idolaters, here one should be stringent — thus it appears to me:`,
    '7#ב': `"And if it is not known etc." For it is a rabbinic safek to be lenient:`,
    '8#_': `"And if the non-Jew swayed." All this is Rambam's words chapter 12 of forbidden foods, and he follows his view that Tur wrote siman 124 in his name — that he holds a non-Jew grasps an open vessel and agitates it and forbids even though he did not lift, as written in Shulchan Aruch siman 124 seif 17; and this is the explanation of what they said in the gemara "and if he sways from side to side — forbidden"; and here Tur also brought this law in this language "and if the gentiles bring the vessel near the splash" — for Rashi forbidden for drinking and for Rivam forbidden in benefit, and likewise is the conclusion of Avi Ezri, end of his language — language of closeness appears also without lifting, for it is possible to bring near without lifting; and therefore one may ask what Tur wrote in siman 124 on Rambam's words that they do not appear correct to Avi Ezri — and Beit Yosef already challenged this; and above I copied in the name of Darkei Moshe that here it deals with lifting — but it does not appear thus to me, as I wrote; and likewise it appears from Rosh who copied Rivam's words on this law that he lifts one side of the vessel etc. — and behold certainly this is not lifting; and it appears to me that above there is no proof that the non-Jew intends in his grasping the vessel that it is for the wine inside, unlike here when he brings it near the splash of wine — for it is proven he acts so that the wine be received from his force. And three disputes on one matter: Rambam holds that in every case where the non-Jew agitates, even if he does not lift, he forbids; Rosh holds there is issur in agitating through a vessel; and in Derishah he wrote that for Rosh there is no forbidding in agitation at all except through touching — and he did not examine Asheri that I mentioned, that is when he lifts it, as Rosh wrote perek R. Yishmael that one must say thus; but without lifting there is no manner of libation — that is wherever there is no proof he acts for the sake of the wine, as I wrote; but here when he brings near the splash of wine he forbids even without lifting; but Rashba and Raavad hold there is no issur in agitation except when he agitates inside the wine vessel with his hand or through something else; and Rashba explained another explanation on that which we say in the gemara "and if he sways from side to side — forbidden" — we are concerned when he sways he touched the walls of the vessel for the splash of wine descending, and it is his contact through something else, and perhaps he intends thus; and for all explanations there is no issur for us since we permit contact of a non-Jew through something else, as he wrote above siman 124:`,
    '9#_': `"The mouth of the wineskin in his hand." This is Rambam's view that one who grasps an open vessel and agitates forbids — until here he must grasp the mouth of the wineskin in his hand so it not be an open vessel so much — Beit Yosef. But I wonder — for it was explained that also Rosh holds he forbids in agitation through lifting, and here it deals with lifting for he carries the wineskin; and it appears since he carries by way of walking it is not called manner of libation thus; and likewise I saw in Derishah in the name of Rashal who wrote that also Rambam agrees here permitted for this reason; but one cannot distinguish thus in Rambam's view — for if so why was it needed here that the mouth of the wineskin be in his hand? Rather for Rosh one may distinguish thus:`,
  },
};
